 nw:{title:'163 First Avenue',corner:'Northwest corner',image:'references/Tarallucci_in_the_Rain_(51286241651).jpg',caption:'2021 · Eden, Janine and Jim · CC BY 2.0',source:'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tarallucci_in_the_Rain_(51286241651).jpg',extra:'https://invest.jll.com/us/en/listings/living-multi-housing/the-east-village-collection',notes:'Three stories. Orange render, two groups of three windows on First Avenue, and a long side along East 10th. This licensed photo shows the former café. The new storefront pass uses Gelatoville and HAGS references; the upper architecture remains based on the full-building reference. Exact storefront partition widths are estimated.'},
 ne:{title:'164 First Avenue',corner:'Northeast corner',image:'references/ne-e10th-side-2022-05-26-1920.jpg',caption:'May 26, 2022 · Kidfly182 · CC BY-SA 4.0',source:'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:East_Village_Manhattan_May_2022_093.jpg',notes:'Four stories with a rooftop antenna rack. The East 10th wall has a blind window bay and an exposed exhaust duct. Beron’s two First Avenue art panels use a separate, licensed May 2025 photograph. The restaurant closed December 31, 2025. Its sign is retained as historical reference; the current successor is unconfirmed.'},
 sw:{title:'159–161 First Avenue',corner:'Southwest corner',image:'references/sw-159-first-2021-09-06.jpg',caption:'September 6, 2021 · Eden, Janine and Jim · CC BY 2.0',source:'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:159_First_Avenue_(51432373044).jpg',notes:'Six stories, pale brick, a deep rust-brown cornice, and fire escapes on both streets. The taller building behind it has a separate, narrow footprint and a stack of projecting balconies. Eye & Health and Apollo Bagels have current listing checks. The theater behind them now has its distinctive red-and-white entrance. Interiors are representative.'},
 se:{title:'162 First Avenue',corner:'Southeast corner',image:'references/First_and_10th_(51645797940).jpg',caption:'October 31, 2021 · Eden, Janine and Jim · CC BY 2.0',source:'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:First_%26_10th_(51645797940).jpg',notes:'Four stories with three windows per residential row and a red fire escape. Its relatively plain brick facade sits beside the much taller, ornate 160 First Avenue building. Nishaan at 160 now uses its blue awning and sandwich sign. The tenant at 162 is unconfirmed; the older deli branding has been removed.'}
